      "text_sha256": "afad1d620f4f5ecd35780f5827fe98f2efa07a986f37763547777d30c99b3fff"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0462-block-0005",
      "order": 5,
      "text": "Stopping these medications is often difficult (Table 3.28). One study found that 90% of \npatients experience withdrawal symptoms on stopping, with 32% of people on long \nhalf-life benzodiazepines and 42% of people on short half-life benzodiazepines unable \nto cease their medication because of withdrawal symptoms.19 Short-acting drugs such \nas lorazepam are associated with more severe problems on withdrawal than longer-\nacting drugs such as diazepam.20,21 As the drugs are ineffective for anxiety and insomnia \nin the long-term, symptoms which arise on stopping are likely to be withdrawal symp-\ntoms as opposed to relapse (though symptoms can be similar).22 Mental state often \nimproves after withdrawal symptoms abate.23\n",
      "bbox": [
        47.9984130859375,
        194.55047607421875,
        422.7983703613281,
        302.2704772949219
      ],
      "text_sha256": "5d26d9520539598d3dcc601b79fdae415bebfb9954f75dc5a6ad71899a6a97c7"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0462-block-0006",
      "order": 6,
      "text": "To avoid or reduce the severity of these problems, good practice dictates that benzo-\ndiazepines (and z-drugs) should not be prescribed as hypnotics or anxiolytics for longer \nthan 4 weeks. Intermittent use (i.e. not every day) at the lowest possible dose is also \nprudent. This may also apply to gabapentinoids.\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.0,
        302.55047607421875,
        422.77996826171875,
        350.2704772949219
      ],
      "text_sha256": "7a6d54de05326c0b4cb042c7770ccc4a5306ad85ec34d6520ce25aba043bd808"
    },
    {
